Comment by mhsatman:

General Comment:
oh ok!

I assumed .Internal(sample(length(x), size, replace, prob)) instead of
x[.Internal(sample(length(x), size, replace, prob))]. That is my fault and
I will correct it :)

Sorry. This was not "nt". Functions:

   f("dnchisq", /*math3*/ null, 34, 11, 3 + 1);
     f("pnchisq", /*math3*/ null, 35, 11, 3 + 2);
     f("qnchisq", /*math3*/ null, 36, 11, 3 + 2);

     f("dnt", /*math3*/ null, 37, 11, 3 + 1);
     f("pnt", /*math3*/ null, 38, 11, 3 + 2);
     f("qnt", /*math3*/ null, 39, 11, 3 + 2);

they must non-central distributions and they are not directly used by the
interpreter. I can search for use of those functions..

finally, everything is ok.

For more information:
http://code.google.com/p/renjin/source/detail?r=358

Comment by al...@bedatadriven.com:

Score: Positive

General Comment:
Ahh ok. d/p/qnt are internal primitives called from the d/p/qt functions:

qt <- function (p, df, ncp, lower.tail = TRUE, log.p = FALSE)
{
     if (missing(ncp))
         .Internal(qt(p, df, lower.tail, log.p))
     else .Internal(qnt(p, df, ncp, lower.tail, log.p))

}

For more information:
http://code.google.com/p/renjin/source/detail?r=358

